WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:05.400
These are Bong Joon Ho’s complete storyboards
for Parasite.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.130
They’re meticulous, and with few exceptions
the film is a pretty direct translation of

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:22.369
them.

00:00:22.369 --> 00:00:50.610
One of the clear things we can see comparing
the storyboards to the film, is that most

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:55.980
of the differences are places where Bong ended
up going with something simpler than his storyboards.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:01:00.460
You can see this in the example I used earlier,
where the storyboard indicates the peach be

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.260
put into a bag while the camera tracks down.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.600
In the film This is simplified by removing
the camera pan.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.219
One reason Bong might have made this change
is if the action of blowing on the peach was

00:01:10.219 --> 00:01:14.790
discovered in the performance, by actress
Park So Dam on set, and simplifying the shot

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:16.939
better showcase that piece of performance.

00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:22.560
Here’s another example of how Bong simplifies
things.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:32.650
In 
this scene Bong eliminates some dialogue,

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:37.030
an entire shot, and simplifies these two shots
into a single shot.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:43.460
BUT, while he’ll make small changes, simplify
things, or eliminate shots, he almost never

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.140
adds something that isn’t in the storyboard.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:54.869
If he does add or change something it’s
usually fairly small details.

00:01:54.869 --> 00:01:59.399
Like moving the arrow the housekeeper grabs
from the wall to above the door or adding

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:04.060
fairly simple shot stationary shot.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:07.680
Bong is giving the actors room to shift some
things in performance.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:12.640
But he’s obviously not doing a lot of improvisational
work on set in terms of coming up with camera

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:13.890
movements or character blocking.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:14.890
This is all established ahead of time.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:19.170
Coming up with something complicated on the
spot is a lot harder than simplifying a complex

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.170
plan.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:23.761
So don’t be afraid to make your scenes as
complex as you want in the storyboards- you

00:02:23.761 --> 00:02:29.470
can always simplify on set.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:33.601
While the camera movements and scenes you’re
storyboarding might be complex, the storyboards

00:02:33.601 --> 00:02:35.530
themselves don’t need to be.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:40.210
Focus on conveying the key elements of the
scene as simply as possible.

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:42.580
Character and Camera movement are the first
priority.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:47.590
But you can also convey a key element of performance
fairly simply.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:52.480
Some lines, or a head turn can indicate surprise
or amazement.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:55.290
Some things might be too tricky to draw, that’s
okay.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:58.340
You can always just use a label.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:01.870
Smaller details like lighting and subtle emotions
you can leave out.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:06.260
And sometimes the environment around the characters
isn’t important to the shot.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:09.480
Especially if this has been previously established
in the storyboards.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.340
Just make sure you have the core elements
of the shot laid out in a clear way.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:18.010
The most important things being the actors
position relative to the camera, their movement,

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:22.400
and the movement of the camera.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.010
But even though you don’t need to include
everything in your storyboards, and keeping

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:31.370
them simple is good, no detail is too subtle
to be included if it’s important.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:40.810
This shot has an extremely subtle camera move,
subtle enough that even though I’ve watched

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:45.510
this film many times, and scrutinized the
cinematography and editing of this scene for

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:50.450
other videos I never noticed the movement
until I saw it noted in the storyboards.

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:59.420
So however subtle a detail, if it’s important
to you, you can include it in the storyboards.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:04.090
In Parasite Bong’s camera is almost always
moving, this movement and how it should be

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:06.900
done is all laid out in the storyboards.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:11.430
Don’t just stop at drawing an arrow that
shows the camera moves- give detail about

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:17.430
how that zoom is going to be done, is the
camera doing the zooming, is it a dolly push,

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:20.439
is it a digital zoom in post?

00:04:20.439 --> 00:04:23.960
The crew should be able to know what camera
equipment they’ll need to pull the shot

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:24.960
off.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:31.460
If a movement is too difficult to draw, you
can add some diagrams to help the crew better

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:33.900
understand how you want to move the camera.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:38.389
Bong does this, especially with the more complex
scenes involving a lot of people.

00:04:38.389 --> 00:04:42.270
And it’s not just for the camera movement,
for the chaotic finale, he includes a diagram

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:48.200
just to show the position of everything and
where all the characters are moving.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.260
Don’t be afraid to whip out that iphone.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:55.919
If you’re planning to shoot in real locations,
and you know what they are already- you might

00:04:55.919 --> 00:04:58.550
as well integrate them into the storyboards.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:03.289
For the mansion, which Bong designed as a
set, he occasionally used 3D renderings to

00:05:03.289 --> 00:05:11.259
show what the house would look like once it
was built.

00:05:11.259 --> 00:05:16.099
If you have stunts or action you have to storyboard
it in a way that’s actually possible.

00:05:16.099 --> 00:05:21.710
For example if you need to swap between stunt
performers and actors you need to plan a cut

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:24.819
into the storyboard to cover up that swap.

00:05:24.819 --> 00:05:29.050
As you draw your storyboards you’re essentially
pre-editing the film, so you have to think

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:32.290
about the things that would usually be covered
over with editing.

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:36.560
It’s possible to storyboard things that
would be impossible to shoot, so make sure

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:38.979
you’re thinking ahead to production and
post-production.

00:05:38.979 --> 00:05:44.650
It’s also important to think about the transitions
between shots and scenes and how you can use

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:46.780
juxtaposition for your story.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:51.479
An edit like this one, that contrasts the
poor family in the dark getting wet, against

00:05:51.479 --> 00:05:54.569
the rich family, high, dry and in the sun-

00:05:54.569 --> 00:05:58.529
or this that contrasts the rich family’s
closet against the poor families trying to

00:05:58.529 --> 00:06:03.050
find clothes- are specifically planned out
in the storyboards.

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:07.969
Finally don’t forget to draw little devil
horns on your characters when they’re being

00:06:07.969 --> 00:06:11.689
bad!

00:06:11.689 --> 00:06:13.710
Storyboards are a powerful tool.

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:18.379
But in the book’s introduction Bong says
that he doesn’t think storyboarding is necessary

00:06:18.379 --> 00:06:20.030
for making good films.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:24.990
Bong worries that being so structured eliminates
some of the magic that comes from spontaneity

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:26.060
on set.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:32.020
He admits that his main motivation for storyboarding
is to alleviate his anxiety.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:36.110
If Oscar winner Bong Joon Ho is anxious about
the work he does, then you and I can take

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:40.960
comfort in the fact having anxiety about our
work, doesn’t mean we don’t have the skill.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:45.889
And if we’re willing, like Bong, we can
find methods that help us overcome that anxiety.

00:06:45.889 --> 00:06:50.259
If meticulously planning things out and then
translating those plans straight to film is

00:06:50.259 --> 00:07:43.779
what works for you then go ahead- Storyboard
like

00:07:43.779 --> 00:07:46.439
Bong Joon-Ho.

